\'s now your turn), so they likely don’t want to waste their rocket or bomb either.\n\nBut wait — the game history shows only Turn 1 by the landlord. Then it\'s your turn. So your teammate hasn\'t played yet — they **passed** after the landlord\'s bomb.\n\nThat means both peasants passed on a bomb — very common early on.\n\nNow it\'s your turn. You can either pass or rocket.\n\nIf you pass, the landlord gets to lead again — dangerous, because they may continue dumping cards.\n\nIf you rocket, you take control and can lead a safe, small play — like a single 3 or a pair of 3s.\n\nGiven that:\n- The game is early.\n- Both peasants passed the bomb.\n- The landlord has 16 cards — still many.\n- You holding the rocket may be the only way to interrupt a potential run.\n\nBest strategy: **Use the rocket now to seize control**, then lead with a low, non-threatening play that your teammate can easily continue or extend, helping them empty their hand.\n\nFor example, after rocketing, play a single 3 or a pair of 3s — minimal value, lets teammate take over if they can.\n\nThus, playing the rocket is justified to disrupt landlord’s momentum and transfer lead to you, so you can strategically feed the teammate.\n\nReason: The landlord played a bomb, showing aggression. Both peasants passed. To prevent the landlord from gaining unchecked control, I must use the rocket to take the lead and shift initiative. With strong remaining cards and the ability to lead a low-value play (e.g., 3), I can cooperate with my teammate. Holding onto the rocket risks wasting its tactical value later if the landlord plays another bomb near the end. Better to use it now to create an opening.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["BlackJoker", "RedJoker"]}'}
